<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tréguier</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tréguier is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern Brittany, where the Jaudy and Guindy rivers meet. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1790, and its Gothic cathedral holds the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-of-kermartin">Saint Yves</a>, who was born nearby. The first Breton dictionary, the Catholicon, was printed there in 1499, and it is the birthplace of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treguier.jpg" alt="Tréguier" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Cotes_d_Armor_Treguier_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
